  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe 250mb jet streak looks to remain oriented northwest to southeast \nacross the western portion of NM throughout the second half of the \nweek. A weak 500mb inverted trough looks to develop within the \nremnants of the high pressure within this time as well. The \ncombination of these two features look to continue the chances of \ndrier showers and storms across the high terrain in western and \nnorthern NM on Wednesday and Thursday. Across Thursday and Friday, \nthe 250mb jet streak begins to develop a trough to the southeast of \nNM, forming either a closed low or open wave at 500mb (models begin \nto differ on the strength of said feature by this point). The \nfavored location of development for this system is near or east of \nthe Big Bend in TX. However, should this feature form further west, \ncannot rule out a few showers making its way into southeast NM. \nThroughout the rest of this week, the persistent northwest flow \naloft should allow for temperatures to gradually decrease across the \narea.\n\nMoving into the weekend, the next feature to keep an eye on will be \na hefty low in the Pacific Northwest. There is currently \nconsiderable timing discrepancies between deterministic models, as \nwell as how strong and sharp it becomes. Looking at ensemble \nclusters, the slightly favored outcome at this time is a sharper, \nstronger system, which works to draw in moisture to the area and \nprovide a more active end to the weekend in terms of precipitation. \nThe slightly less favored solution is the low remaining weaker and \nmore confined to the PNW. In this case, less moisture would be \nadvected into the region. Both solutions point towards the potential \nof breezier conditions across the region.",
